<%# One remembered change's effect on a record the page is showing, as one badge.
    #
    # Three states, and they are three different sentences because three different
    # things are true: a `draft` row would not exist until the draft is applied, an
    # `edit` row is showing values somebody else may change, and a `deletion` row is
    # about to stop existing.
    #
    # **It takes the record and reads both answers itself.** It used to take a
    # `state` the caller had computed, which meant fifteen call sites each carrying
    # a badge's word without its explanation — and a caller that passed a state and
    # no record had nowhere to put the explanation at all. One local is the whole
    # contract now, and the word and the sentence cannot drift apart.
    #
    # **The `title` is where the badge is allowed to be specific.** The row
    # underneath is unchanged — a remembered edit shows the values that are stored
    # today, and a remembered delete shows the record as it is until the draft is
    # applied — so the badge word alone is a claim the reader has to take on trust.
    # `DraftsHelper#draft_pending_title` is what makes it checkable: it names the
    # state, and for more than one remembered change it also names the fields an
    # edit would write and how many changes are waiting. A record with three edits
    # on three fields carries one badge, and the tooltip is what says so.
    #
    # `draft_pending_state` serves a stored row and a pending one from the same
    # question, which is why one partial covers both: an unsaved record is `:draft`
    # by construction, and a stored one is whatever the reader's draft holds on it. %>
<% state = draft_pending_state(record) %>
<% if state.present? %>
  <span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-warning border draft-pending-badge"
    title="<%= draft_pending_title(record) %>">
    <i class="bi <%= t("drafts.pending.states.#{state}_icon") %>" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    <%= t("drafts.pending.states.#{state}") %>
  </span>
<% end %>
